      "realLife": "One printing machine with three output trays: print to the screen, print onto a page in a file, or print onto a sticker you keep in your pocket (a string). Same ink, same format, different destination.",
      "code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int   roll  = 21;\n    float marks = 87.5;\n    char  line[50];\n    int   a;\n    float b;\n\n    // console\n    printf(\"%-6d|%8.2f|\\n\", roll, marks);   // width + precision\n\n    // into a STRING in memory\n    sprintf(line, \"Roll %d scored %.2f\", roll, marks);\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", line);                   // Roll 21 scored 87.50\n\n    // out of a STRING in memory\n    sscanf(\"45 60.5\", \"%d %f\", &a, &b);\n    printf(\"%d %.1f\\n\", a, b);              // 45 60.5\n\n    // the & rule\n    int n;\n    char name[20];\n    // scanf(\"%d\", &n);      // & is needed for a plain variable\n    // scanf(\"%s\", name);    // NO & - an array name is already an address\n    (void)n; (void)name;\n    return 0;\n}",
      "codeNote": "sprintf prints INTO a string and sscanf reads OUT of one — the same format rules, only the destination changes.",
      "mistake": "Writing <code>scanf(\"%d\", n);</code> without the <code>&amp;</code>. scanf then treats the value of n as an address and writes there, which usually crashes the program. Rule: <code>&amp;</code> for plain variables, no <code>&amp;</code> for arrays and strings.",
      "examAnswer": "The printf and scanf family are the formatted input and output functions of C, all of which use a format string containing conversion specifiers such as %d, %f, %c and %s. The functions printf() and scanf() operate on the standard output and standard input respectively, fprintf() and fscanf() perform the same formatted operations on a file identified by a file pointer given as the first argument, and sprintf() and sscanf() operate on a character array in memory rather than on a stream. The scanf family requires the address of each variable to be supplied using the & operator, except for arrays, whose name already denotes the address of the first element. printf() returns the number of characters written, while scanf() returns the number of input items successfully assigned.",
      "remember": "No letter = screen, f = file, s = string. &amp; for variables, no &amp; for arrays.",
      "why": "Formatted input and output is how every C program talks to the user and to files; sprintf is also the standard way to build a message or a filename at run time.",
      "practice": "Use sprintf to build the string \"Student 21 got 87.50 marks\" and then use sscanf to extract the number 21 back out of it."
