    {
      "title": "The C Preprocessor: #include, #define and #undef",
      "teacher": "Here is something most students never realise: before the compiler ever sees your program, another program has already rewritten it. It obeys the lines starting with a hash. Let's meet this silent editor.",
      "explain": "The <strong>preprocessor</strong> is a program that runs <strong>before compilation</strong>. It obeys every line that begins with <code>#</code> — these are <strong>directives</strong>, not C statements, so they take <strong>no semicolon</strong>. It does pure <strong>text substitution</strong>: <code>#include</code> pastes in the contents of a header file, <code>#define</code> replaces a name with its text everywhere, and <code>#undef</code> cancels a definition.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "The preprocessor runs <strong>before</strong> the compiler; the compiler never sees the <code>#</code> lines.",
        "Directives start with <code>#</code> and take <strong>no semicolon</strong> at the end.",
        "<code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;</code> searches the system folders; <code>#include \"my.h\"</code> searches your folder first.",
        "<strong>Object-like macro</strong>: <code>#define PI 3.14159</code> — a simple name replaced by text.",
        "<strong>Function-like macro</strong>: <code>#define SQUARE(x) ((x)*(x))</code> — takes arguments.",
        "A macro is <strong>text replacement, not a function call</strong> — no type checking, no return value.",
        "<code>#undef PI</code> removes a definition so the name can be reused.",
        "Convention: write macro names in CAPITALS so they are visible in the code."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "The four stages before your program runs",
          "text": "<strong>(1) Preprocessing</strong> — the <code>#</code> lines are carried out, producing an expanded source file. <strong>(2) Compilation</strong> — that expanded file becomes assembly and then object code. <strong>(3) Linking</strong> — your object code is joined with library code such as printf. <strong>(4) Loading</strong> — the final executable is placed in memory and run. Every one of those stages can produce its own kind of error."
        },
        {
          "title": "Macro vs function",
          "text": "A <strong>macro</strong> is expanded in place, so it is faster (no call overhead) but the code is duplicated everywhere and there is no type checking — errors show up in the expanded text and are confusing. A <strong>function</strong> is called at run time, is type checked and exists once in memory. Use macros for short constants and tiny expressions; use functions for real logic."
        },
        {
          "title": "The classic bracket bug",
          "text": "<code>#define SQUARE(x) x*x</code> looks fine, but <code>SQUARE(2+3)</code> is replaced by the text <code>2+3*2+3</code>, which is 2 + 6 + 3 = <strong>11</strong>, not 25. The preprocessor pastes text; it does not understand arithmetic. Always wrap both the parameters and the whole body in brackets: <code>#define SQUARE(x) ((x)*(x))</code>."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "A proof-reader who goes through your handwritten assignment before the examiner and replaces every 'CM' with 'centimetre'. She does not understand the physics — she only swaps the words. That is exactly how literal the preprocessor is.",
      "worked": "<strong>Why SQUARE(2+3) gives 11 instead of 25.</strong><br><br><strong>Wrong macro:</strong> <code>#define SQUARE(x) x*x</code><br>Source line: <code>int r = SQUARE(2+3);</code><br><strong>Step 1</strong> — the preprocessor puts the text <code>2+3</code> wherever x appears:<br><code>int r = 2+3*2+3;</code><br><strong>Step 2</strong> — now the compiler applies normal precedence: <code>*</code> before <code>+</code><br><code>= 2 + (3*2) + 3</code><br><code>= 2 + 6 + 3</code><br><strong>= 11</strong>&nbsp; (wrong!)<br><br><strong>Correct macro:</strong> <code>#define SQUARE(x) ((x)*(x))</code><br><strong>Step 1</strong> — substitution:<br><code>int r = ((2+3)*(2+3));</code><br><strong>Step 2</strong> — brackets force the addition first:<br><code>= (5 * 5)</code><br><strong>= 25</strong>&nbsp; (correct)<br><br><strong>Lesson:</strong> the preprocessor pastes TEXT, not values. Bracket every parameter and bracket the whole body.",
      "code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\n#define PI 3.14159                 // object-like macro\n#define MAX 100                    // no semicolon, no data type\n\n#define BAD_SQ(x)  x*x             // WRONG - no brackets\n#define SQUARE(x) ((x)*(x))        // CORRECT - everything bracketed\n#define BIGGER(a,b) ((a) > (b) ? (a) : (b))\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"Area   = %.2f\\n\", PI * 2 * 2);     // 12.57\n\n    printf(\"BAD_SQ(2+3) = %d\\n\", BAD_SQ(2+3)); // 11  <- the bug\n    printf(\"SQUARE(2+3) = %d\\n\", SQUARE(2+3)); // 25  <- correct\n    printf(\"BIGGER(4,9) = %d\\n\", BIGGER(4,9)); // 9\n\n#undef MAX                         // cancel the definition\n#define MAX 500                    // now it can be redefined safely\n    printf(\"MAX = %d\\n\", MAX);                 // 500\n    return 0;\n}",
      "codeNote": "BAD_SQ(2+3) expands to 2+3*2+3 = 11; SQUARE(2+3) expands to ((2+3)*(2+3)) = 25.",
      "mistake": "Putting a semicolon on a directive: <code>#define MAX 100;</code>. The semicolon becomes part of the replacement text, so <code>int a[MAX];</code> expands to <code>int a[100;];</code> and gives a strange error far away from the real cause.",
      "examAnswer": "The C preprocessor is a program that processes the source code before compilation begins. It acts upon all lines beginning with the symbol #, which are called preprocessor directives and are not terminated by a semicolon. The directive #include inserts the contents of a specified header file into the source code, using angle brackets for system headers and double quotes for user-defined headers. The directive #define creates a macro, which may be object-like, such as #define PI 3.14159, or function-like, such as #define SQUARE(x) ((x)*(x)). Because a macro is expanded by textual substitution and not evaluated as a function, its parameters and body must be fully parenthesised; otherwise an expression such as SQUARE(2+3) defined as x*x expands to 2+3*2+3 and incorrectly yields 11 instead of 25. The directive #undef cancels a previously defined macro.",
      "remember": "The preprocessor runs FIRST and only swaps text. No semicolon on a #line. Bracket every macro parameter.",
      "why": "Macros appear in every real C header file, and 'what is the output of SQUARE(2+3)' is one of the most commonly asked output questions in this unit.",
      "practice": "Given #define CUBE(x) x*x*x, what does CUBE(1+2) actually expand to and what value does it print? Now write the corrected macro."
    },
    {
      "title": "Conditional Compilation and Include Guards",
      "teacher": "Imagine one answer sheet that automatically shows the Hindi version to one examiner and the English version to another — without rewriting it. Conditional compilation does that for your source code, choosing which lines even reach the compiler.",
      "explain": "<strong>Conditional compilation</strong> lets you include or exclude parts of your source code depending on a condition tested at preprocessing time. The directives are <code>#if</code>, <code>#else</code>, <code>#elif</code>, <code>#endif</code>, <code>#ifdef</code> and <code>#ifndef</code>. The lines that fail the test are <strong>deleted before the compiler ever sees them</strong>, so they cost nothing at run time. Its most important everyday use is the <strong>include guard</strong>, which stops a header file from being included twice.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<code>#if</code> tests a constant expression; <code>#elif</code> is 'else if'; <code>#else</code> is the fallback.",
        "<strong>Every <code>#if</code>, <code>#ifdef</code> or <code>#ifndef</code> must be closed with <code>#endif</code></strong> — there are no braces.",
        "<code>#ifdef NAME</code> — true if NAME has been defined (whatever its value).",
        "<code>#ifndef NAME</code> — true if NAME has <strong>not</strong> been defined.",
        "<code>#if defined(NAME)</code> is the same as <code>#ifdef NAME</code>, but can be combined with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code>.",
        "The rejected code is <strong>removed before compilation</strong> — it does not even need to be valid C.",
        "Uses: debug messages on/off, different code for Windows and Linux, turning features on or off.",
        "<strong>Include guard</strong>: <code>#ifndef X_H</code> / <code>#define X_H</code> / ... / <code>#endif</code> prevents double inclusion."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "Why include guards are needed",
          "text": "If <code>main.c</code> includes both <code>student.h</code> and <code>result.h</code>, and <code>result.h</code> also includes <code>student.h</code>, then the structure declaration arrives twice and the compiler reports 'redefinition'. An include guard fixes it: the first time, <code>X_H</code> is undefined so the body is included and <code>X_H</code> is defined; the second time <code>#ifndef</code> is false and the whole body is skipped."
        },
        {
          "title": "Debug switches",
          "text": "Wrapping your tracing messages in <code>#ifdef DEBUG ... #endif</code> means you keep them in the source for ever, but they vanish completely from the final program when you remove the <code>#define DEBUG</code> line. Since the code is deleted before compilation, there is <strong>zero</strong> speed cost — unlike an <code>if</code> statement, which is still tested every time it runs."
        },
        {
          "title": "#if versus if",
          "text": "They look similar but happen at different times. <code>#if</code> is decided by the <strong>preprocessor</strong>, before compiling, using only constants and macros; the losing branch is thrown away and never becomes part of the program. A normal <code>if</code> is decided by the <strong>CPU while the program runs</strong>, and both branches are compiled into the executable."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "A question paper printed with the instruction 'Attempt section A if you are from the CS branch, otherwise section B'. Conditional compilation goes one step further — the printer simply does not print the section you are not supposed to attempt.",
      "code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\n#define DEBUG\n#define VERSION 2\n\nint main() {\n#ifdef DEBUG                       // true because DEBUG is defined\n    printf(\"Debug mode is ON\\n\");\n#endif\n\n#if VERSION == 1\n    printf(\"Version 1\\n\");\n#elif VERSION == 2\n    printf(\"Version 2\\n\");        // this line is chosen\n#else\n    printf(\"Unknown version\\n\");\n#endif\n\n#ifndef MAX                        // true - MAX is not defined yet\n#define MAX 100\n#endif\n    printf(\"MAX = %d\\n\", MAX);    // 100\n    return 0;\n}\n\n/* A standard include guard, used at the top of every header file:\n\n   #ifndef STUDENT_H\n   #define STUDENT_H\n   struct Student { int roll; char name[20]; };\n   #endif\n*/",
      "codeNote": "Output is exactly three lines: Debug mode is ON / Version 2 / MAX = 100 — the other branches never reach the compiler.",
      "mistake": "Forgetting <code>#endif</code>. Unlike <code>if</code>, a preprocessor conditional has no closing brace, so the preprocessor keeps swallowing the rest of your file and you get a confusing error at the very last line.",
      "examAnswer": "Conditional compilation allows selected portions of the source code to be included in or excluded from compilation, based on conditions evaluated by the preprocessor. The directive #if tests a constant expression, #elif provides an alternative test, #else supplies a default, and every such conditional block must be terminated by #endif. The directive #ifdef is true if the given macro has been defined, and #ifndef is true if it has not. Because the rejected code is removed before the compiler processes the file, it contributes nothing to the size or speed of the executable. The most important application is the include guard, written as #ifndef HEADER_H, #define HEADER_H, the contents of the header, and #endif, which prevents a header file from being included more than once and thereby avoids redefinition errors.",
      "remember": "#ifdef = 'is it defined?'. #ifndef = 'is it NOT defined?'. Every one needs an #endif.",
      "why": "Include guards appear at the top of literally every header file you will ever read, and conditional compilation is how one source file supports many machines.",
      "practice": "Write an include guard for a header named marks.h containing a structure Marks. Then explain in two lines what goes wrong without it."
    },
    {
      "title": "Variable Argument List Functions (stdarg.h)",
      "teacher": "Have you noticed something odd about printf? It takes one argument sometimes and six other times, and never complains. How is that even possible in C, where every function declares its parameters? Here is the secret.",
      "explain": "A <strong>variadic function</strong> accepts a variable number of arguments. It is declared with at least one fixed parameter followed by three dots — the <strong>ellipsis</strong> <code>...</code> — as in <code>int addAll(int count, ...)</code>. The extra arguments are read using four tools from <code>&lt;stdarg.h&gt;</code>: the type <code>va_list</code> and the macros <code>va_start</code>, <code>va_arg</code> and <code>va_end</code>.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "Needs <code>#include &lt;stdarg.h&gt;</code>.",
        "There must be <strong>at least one fixed parameter</strong> before the <code>...</code>.",
        "The <code>...</code> must always be the <strong>last</strong> parameter.",
        "<code>va_list ap;</code> — declares the pointer that walks through the extra arguments.",
        "<code>va_start(ap, last)</code> — start, naming the <strong>last fixed</strong> parameter.",
        "<code>va_arg(ap, type)</code> — fetch the next argument; you must state its type yourself.",
        "<code>va_end(ap)</code> — clean up before returning. Never skip it.",
        "C cannot count the extra arguments, so you must pass a count (like printf's format string does)."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "The four steps, always in this order",
          "text": "<strong>(1)</strong> Declare <code>va_list ap;</code>. <strong>(2)</strong> Call <code>va_start(ap, count)</code>, naming the last fixed parameter so the macro knows where the extras begin. <strong>(3)</strong> Call <code>va_arg(ap, int)</code> once for every extra argument, in order. <strong>(4)</strong> Call <code>va_end(ap)</code> before returning. Missing step 4 is undefined behaviour."
        },
        {
          "title": "How the function knows when to stop",
          "text": "C gives the function <strong>no way</strong> to count the extra arguments, so you must tell it. Two standard tricks: pass a count as the first fixed parameter, as <code>addAll(3, 10, 20, 30)</code> does; or use a sentinel value such as <code>-1</code> or <code>NULL</code> to mark the end. <code>printf</code> uses a third method — it counts the <code>%</code> specifiers in the format string."
        },
        {
          "title": "The danger of no type checking",
          "text": "<code>va_arg(ap, int)</code> simply trusts you. If the caller actually passed a double and you ask for an int, you get garbage and possibly a crash — the compiler cannot warn you, because it does not know what was passed. This is exactly why <code>printf(\"%d\", 3.5)</code> prints nonsense instead of giving a compile error."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "A shared auto-rickshaw. It does not have a fixed number of seats declared in advance — the driver takes as many passengers as turn up, and the first passenger tells him how many are coming so he knows when to leave.",
      "code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdarg.h>              // needed for va_list and friends\n\nint addAll(int count, ...) {      // 'count' is fixed, ... means more follow\n    va_list ap;                   // 1. declare\n    int sum = 0, i;\n\n    va_start(ap, count);          // 2. start, naming the LAST fixed parameter\n    for (i = 0; i < count; i++)\n        sum += va_arg(ap, int);   // 3. fetch the next argument as an int\n    va_end(ap);                   // 4. clean up\n\n    return sum;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", addAll(3, 10, 20, 30));       // 60\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", addAll(5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5));    // 15\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", addAll(0));                   // 0\n    return 0;\n}",
      "codeNote": "The first argument tells the function how many numbers follow — without it, C has no way to know where to stop.",
      "mistake": "Writing <code>int addAll(...)</code> with no fixed parameter at all. C requires at least one named parameter before the ellipsis, because <code>va_start</code> needs a starting landmark. The function also has no way of knowing how many arguments arrived.",
      "examAnswer": "A variable argument list function, or variadic function, is a function that can accept a varying number of arguments. It is declared with one or more fixed parameters followed by an ellipsis, for example int addAll(int count, ...), where the ellipsis must be the final parameter and at least one fixed parameter is mandatory. The additional arguments are accessed using the facilities declared in stdarg.h: an object of type va_list is declared, va_start(ap, last) initialises it using the name of the last fixed parameter, va_arg(ap, type) retrieves each subsequent argument according to the type specified, and va_end(ap) performs the required clean-up before the function returns. Since the language provides no mechanism for counting the arguments actually passed, the number must be conveyed by a fixed parameter or by a terminating sentinel value, as printf() does through its format string.",
      "remember": "list, start, arg, end — in that order. Always one fixed parameter before the three dots.",
      "why": "This explains how printf and scanf themselves work, which is a favourite viva question, and it lets you write flexible utility functions of your own.",
      "practice": "Write a variadic function largest(int count, ...) that returns the biggest of however many integers are passed to it."
    }
